"Song of Silence" by Nils Prior

[=== KINDS & PROPERTIES ===]


[=== GLOBAL ACTIONS ===]
Understand "light [something]" as burning.

Understand the command "read" as something new.

Reading is an action applying to one thing.
Understand "read [something]" as reading.

Before reading something:
	if the noun is in a container and the noun is not carried by the player:
		say "[italic type](You take [the noun] out of [the holder of the noun].)[roman type][line break]";
		now the noun is carried by the player.

[=== WORLD ===]

Book - Introduction

Chapter - Apartment

Section - Apartment Variables

[=== APARTMENT (STUDY, HALLWAY, FOYER, BATHROOM, BEDROOM, KITCHEN, DININGROOM, PANTRY ===]

[=== APARTMENT SCOPE VARIABLES ===]
Table of StreetSounds
streetSoundSnippet(text)
"From the street below, the sound of footsteps on cobblestones."
"A car passes in the street, its engine fading into the distance."
"The distant sound of a tram."
"The distant sound of a church bell."
"A child's voice from the street below, then silence."
"The wind moves through the street outside."

Table of StaircaseSounds
staircaseSoundSnippet(text)
"From the staircase, the creak of a floorboard."
"Footsteps on the stairs, then silence."
"Muffled voices from the staircase, too quiet to make out."
"A neighbour's door, somewhere above."
"A distant door, somewhere in the building."
"Someone passes in the stairwell without stopping."

[=== APARTMENT SOUNDS ===]
Every turn when the player is not in the Study and the player is not in the Foyer and the player is not in the Hallway:
	if a random chance of 1 in 5 succeeds:
		choose a random row in the Table of StreetSounds;
		say "[streetSoundSnippet entry][line break]"
		
Every turn when the player is in the Foyer:
	if a random chance of 1 in 4 succeeds:
		choose a random row in the Table of StaircaseSounds;
		say "[staircaseSoundSnippet entry][line break]"

Section - Study

[=== LOCAL VARIABLES ===]
Table of FireplaceSounds
fireSoundSnippet(text)
"In the fireplace the logs crackle softly."
"A few sparks fly up the chimney."
"From the fireplace you hear soft hisses and pops."
"The flames lick slowly at the logs."
"A small log shifts with a soft thud."
"The fire pops, sending a glow across the walls."
"In the fireplace the embers glow a deep, steady red."

[=== DESCRIPTION ===]

Study is a room. "Study is a room. Your study is a small but comfortable room. Dark wooden floorboards creak softly underfoot. Opposite the door, a brick fireplace dominates the wall[if the fireplace is lit], a fire crackling quietly in the grate[otherwise], its grate neatly laid with logs and kindling[end if]. To your left, a sturdy desk holds a neat arrangement of papers and writing utensils on a leather blotter, a bookshelf beside it, a window above. A well-worn chair stands before it. To your right, a tall grandfather clock stands against the wall, its steady tick a quiet presence in the room. On the walls hang several framed pictures. A door leads west to the foyer."
Study is east of Foyer.

[=== ITEMS ===]

The fireplace is undescribed.
The fireplace is either lit or unlit. The fireplace is unlit.
The description of the fireplace is "[if the fireplace is lit]A small fire crackles softly on a bed of logs, the stones around it blackened by years of use. A metal fireguard stands before the opening, catching sparks before they reach the floorboards.[otherwise]A neat arrangement of logs and kindling in the grate, ready to be lit.[end if][if lighter is on fireplace] A lighter rests on the mantelpiece above.[end if]"

The lighter is undescribed.
The description of the lighter is "A plain metal lighter, well-used."
Understand "lighter" or "light" as the lighter.

Instead of burning the fireplace:
	if the player carries the lighter:
		now the fireplace is lit;
		say "You touch the lighter to the kindling. The fire catches quickly, and the room settles into a familiar warmth. You watch it for a moment before turning back to your work.";
	otherwise:
		say "You would need something to light it with."

The paper envelope is an openable container.
The paper envelope is closed.
The paper envelope is undescribed.
The description of the envelope is "The envelope is made from coarse, heavy paper of high quality, hinting at its importance. [if paper envelope is closed]It is sealed by a thick red wax seal.[otherwise]The wax seal is broken.[end if]"

Instead of opening the paper envelope:
now the paper envelope is open;
say "You break the wax seal on the envelope and open it. Inside you see a handwritten letter[line break]"

Check closing the paper envelope:
say "[if paper envelope is open]The wax seal is broken and the heavy paper is too stiff to stay shut on its own.[otherwise]The envelope is already closed. In fact, an unbroken wax seal indicates it has not been openend yet." instead.

The red wax seal is part of the paper envelope.
The red wax seal is undescribed.
The description of the red wax seal is "[if paper envelope is closed]A thick red wax seal bearing a personal signet – the initials A.B. set beneath a small heraldic device.[otherwise]The red wax seal on the envelope is broken.[end if]"

The description of the handwritten letter is "Written in ink with a delicate handwriting. You notice dots of ink on several letters where the writer seems to have paused for a moment."

Instead of reading the handwritten letter:
	say "[italic type]Toledo, 5th October 1938[paragraph break]Dear friend,[paragraph break]I hope this letter finds you well. I write to you in your capacity as a scholar of medieval German texts -- I have come across something in the cathedral archive here that I believe would interest you considerably.[paragraph break]Among the documents I have been cataloguing, I found a manuscript I cannot yet date with precision, though it appears to be from the early thirteenth century. It is written in Middle High German, by a monk from Regensburg, and it describes an incident at the ford of the Tagus river -- unusual in content, historically significant, and in certain details quite unlike anything I have encountered before. I would prefer to discuss it in person rather than commit the details to a letter.[paragraph break]I should also mention that I have a personal reason for wanting to speak with someone I trust. There is a matter from 1936 -- something I witnessed during the siege here -- which I have never spoken of to anyone. I find myself thinking of it again.[paragraph break]If you can arrange to come to Toledo, I would be most grateful. Ask for me at the cathedral. I will explain everything when you arrive.[paragraph break]With collegial regards,[line break]Arturo Belmonte[roman type][paragraph break][italic type]P.S. Toledo is not quite what it was. You will understand when you arrive.[line break][roman type]"

The old clock is undescribed.
The description of the old clock is "Behind the glass panel, a brass pendulum swings in long, unhurried arcs – patient, indifferent, as it has always been. The hands show a quarter past six."

The wooden desk is a supporter.
It is undescribed.
The description of the wooden desk is "A sturdy, wooden desk.[if the paper envelope is on the wooden desk] An envelope of coarse paper sits at the center.[end if][if the fountain pen is on the wooden desk] A fountain pen rests beside the blotter.[end if] A well-worn chair stands before it."

The fountain pen is undescribed.
The description of the fountain pen is "A well-used fountain pen, its nib slightly worn. The ink is dark blue."
Understand "pen" or "fountain pen" as the fountain pen.

The chair is a supporter.
The chair is undescribed.
The description of the chair is "The leather seat is cracked and soft from years of use.[if the tweed jacket is on the chair] A tweed jacket hangs over the back.[end if][if the homburg is on the chair] A homburg hat rests on the seat.[end if]"

The tweed jacket is a container.
The tweed jacket is wearable.
The tweed jacket is undescribed.
The description of the tweed jacket is "A well-worn brown tweed jacket, the elbows slightly shiny from years of use."
Understand "jacket" or "tweed" as the tweed jacket.

The homburg hat is undescribed.
The homburg hat is wearable.
The description of the homburg hat is "A dark grey homburg, brushed clean and well-kept. The hat of a man who takes some pride in his appearance, even in difficult times."
Understand "hat" or "homburg" as the homburg.

The pocket calendar is undescribed.
The description of the pocket calendar is "A small leather-bound pocket calendar. The current date is marked: October 14th, 1938."
Understand "calendar" or "pocket calendar" or "notebook" as the pocket calendar.

The description of the wall pictures is "A group photograph, slightly faded, hangs beside the door. Next to it, a small framed photograph of a young woman. Above the fireplace, an old engraving of Toledo."
Understand "pictures", "picture", "photographs", "frames", "framed" as the wall pictures.

The description of the group photograph is "A photograph, slightly faded. A group of academics stands before what appears to be a Roman ruin, squinting into the sun. You are somewhere in the back row."
Understand "group photograph", "group photo", "academics", "faded photograph" as the group photograph.

The description of the small photograph is "A small framed photograph of a young woman. You do not look at it for long."
Understand "small photograph", "small photo", "woman", "young woman", "framed photograph" as the small photograph.

The description of the engraving is "An old engraving of Toledo, seen from across the Tajo. The city looks much as it must have looked for centuries."
Understand "engraving", "Toledo", "old engraving" as the engraving.

The bookshelf is an open container.
The bookshelf is undescribed.
The bookshelf is fixed in place.
The bookshelf is not openable.
The description of the bookshelf is "Rows of books, ordered and familiar. The collection of many years of research and travel."
Understand "shelf" as the bookshelf.

The printed name of the Dozy is "Histoire des Musulmans d'Espagne".
The description of the Dozy is "Reinhart Dozy's Histoire des Musulmans d'Espagne. A standard work, heavily annotated in pencil – your own handwriting fills the margins."
The Dozy is proper-named.

The printed name of the Caesarius is "Dialogus Miraculorum".
The description of the Caesarius is "Caesarius von Heisterbach's Dialogus Miraculorum, a collection of medieval miracle stories. A bookmark sits somewhere in the middle."
The Caesarius is proper-named.

The printed name of the Baedeker is "Spanien und Portugal".
The description of the Baedeker is "Karl Baedeker's Spanien und Portugal. The spine is cracked from repeated use. Toledo has several pages marked."
The Baedeker is proper-named.

The printed name of the Rilke is "Duineser Elegien".
The description of the Rilke is "A slim volume, its cover worn soft. Rainer Maria Rilke's Duineser Elegien. It sits between the reference works like something that doesn't quite belong."
The Rilke is proper-named.

Instead of reading the Rilke:
	say "You open [italic type]Duineser Elegien[roman type]. The spine falls open naturally at a familiar page – the binding worn soft from years of use.[paragraph break][italic type]Wer, wenn ich schriee, hörte mich denn aus der Engel Ordnungen?[paragraph break][roman type]You turn the page. There is a name written inside the cover, in handwriting that is not your own. You close the book."

Understand "spanien und portugal", "spanien" as the Baedeker.
Understand "histoire des musulmans d'espagne", "histoire", "musulmans" as the Dozy.
Understand "dialogus miraculorum", "dialogus" as the Caesarius.
Understand "duineser elegien", "duineser", "elegien" as the Rilke.

The printed name of the study-window is "window".
Understand "window" as the study-window.
The description of the study-window is "A double-casement window with a wooden frame, overlooking the street below. The evening light falls soft and golden over the rooftops. Below, a few pedestrians make their way home, collars turned up against the autumn chill. It is, by all appearances, a perfectly ordinary evening in Munich."

[=== SOUNDS ===]
Every turn when the player is in the Study:
	if the fireplace is lit:
		if a random chance of 1 in 4 succeeds:
			choose a random row in the Table of FireplaceSounds;
			say "[fireSoundSnippet entry][line break]";
	if the remainder after dividing the turn count by 3 is 0:
		say "[one of]Tick. Tock.[or]The clock marks another minute.[or]The clock's pendulum swings, unhurried.[or]The clock ticks on, indifferent.[or]A soft click from the grandfather clock.[cycling][line break]"
		
